 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DXzcAmxjlOW/",
 "title": "Master Small Talk in an Interview",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator, a 20-plus-year recruiting leader, explains that hiring managers decide if they like a candidate within the first 30 to 45 seconds based heavily on likability and culture fit. She advises using geography and weather for small talk to calm nerves and avoid awkward, forced conversation.",
 "key_points": [
  "A hiring manager knows whether they like you within the first 30 to 45 seconds of an interview.",
  "A huge portion of hiring decisions is based on likability, culture fit, and personality.",
  "You were invited to the interview because your resume proves you can do the job.",
  "Mastering small talk helps you and the hiring manager get to know each other.",
  "The creator's go-to small talk topics are your current geographical location and the weather.",
  "Talking about geography and weather helps your brain settle and your nerves dissipate.",
  "A bad example of small talk sounds forced and robotic, like dragging answers out of someone."
 ],
 "content": "You've got to learn to master small talk for an interview #interview #interviewtips #careercoach #careeradvice #jobsearch\n\nIf you are in an interview, there is one thing I need you to do. You have got to master the small talk. So who am I? 20 plus year recruiting leader. I have interviewed tens of thousands of people in my career. I have sat in executive meetings where hiring decisions are made. And I am telling you that within the first 30 to 45 seconds, a hiring manager knows whether they like you or not. And you can say what you will about an interview, a huge portion of this is likability. Do we think you are a good fit for our organization? Can we see you fitting in with the current team that we have right now? And there is a personality piece of that that they are really looking for. You got invited to the interview because they know you can do the job. They can tell from your resume. You're sitting in front of a hiring manager and a recruiter has already spoken really great things about your background. You have got to master the small talk. This is how we get to know each other. Um my goal, because it's just easy, is talking about where are you geographically and what is the weather like? Call it crazy, but it's an easy thing for me to remember. And as I get into it, the conversation, my brain starts to settle just a little bit and the nerves start to dissipate just a hair. The problem is that when people don't do small talk, this is what it sounds like. Hi Melissa, how are you? Good. Where are you located? Austin. What's the weather like? It's sunny today. That is a horrible way for us to get to know each other and it is like dragging things out of somebody. And it is the first way that a interview will go south. So I hope that that is helpful. Drop into the comments any questions you have and certainly good luck out there.\n\nText on screen: MASTER SMALL TALK IN AN INTERVIEW, LET'S WORK 1:1, SAVE FOR LATER",
